model_response:
Yes—the original answer is correct. We can verify by multiplying:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\-8&5\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}7\\8\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}-14\\-16\end{pmatrix}
=-2\begin{pmatrix}7\\8\end{pmatrix},
\]

and

\[
\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\-8&5\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}0\\5\end{pmatrix}
=5\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}.
\]

So the eigenvectors are all **nonzero multiples** of \(\begin{pmatrix}7\\8\end{pmatrix}\) for \(\lambda=-2\), and of \(\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}\) for \(\lambda=5\). Different-looking scalar multiples are equally valid.

