model_response:
Let’s verify directly. An eigenvector \(v\ne0\) must satisfy \(Av=\lambda v\).

For \(v=\begin{pmatrix}7\\8\end{pmatrix}\),
\[
\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\-8&5\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}7\\8\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}-14\\-16\end{pmatrix}
=-2\begin{pmatrix}7\\8\end{pmatrix}.
\]

For \(v=\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}\),
\[
\begin{pmatrix}-2&0\\-8&5\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}0\\5\end{pmatrix}
=5\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Thus the original answer is correct under the standard convention of **right eigenvectors**. Eigenvectors can be scaled by any nonzero constant, so a reference may give equivalent vectors that look different. If you share the cited result, I can help reconcile the discrepancy.

