(\sqrt{14}-\sqrt{65})\sqrt{133}
=\sqrt{1862}-\sqrt{8645}
=\boxed{7\sqrt{38}-\sqrt{8645}},
\]
since \(1862=49\cdot38\) and \(8645=5\cdot7\cdot13\cdot19\) has no perfect-square factors greater than \(1\).

ground_truth:
Step 1:
\begin{array}{l}

\begin{array}{l}
 \text{Simplify the following}: \\
 \left(\sqrt{14}-\sqrt{65}\right) \sqrt{133} \\
\end{array}
Step 2:
\begin{array}{l}
 \sqrt{133} \left(\sqrt{14}-\sqrt{65}\right)=\sqrt{133} \sqrt{14}+\sqrt{133} \left(-\sqrt{65}\right): \\
 \sqrt{133} \sqrt{14}-\sqrt{133} \sqrt{65} \\
\end{array}
Step 3:
\begin{array}{l}
 \sqrt{133} \sqrt{14}=\sqrt{133\times 14}: \\
 \fbox{$\sqrt{133\times 14}$}-\sqrt{133} \sqrt{65} \\
\end{array}
Step 4:
\begin{array}{l}
 133\times

