model_response:
Yes—the answer is correct. We can verify it by multiplication:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}-3&-3\\1&5\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}-\frac5{12}&-\frac14\\[2pt]\frac1{12}&\frac14\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{15-3}{12}&\frac{3-3}{4}\\[2pt]
\frac{-5+5}{12}&\frac{-1+5}{4}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Since the matrix is invertible, this is also its Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse.

