model_response:
For the matrix as written, the previous answer is correct. A direct check gives
\[
\begin{pmatrix}4&5\\-1&-3\end{pmatrix}
\frac17\begin{pmatrix}3&5\\-1&-4\end{pmatrix}
=\frac17\begin{pmatrix}7&0\\0&7\end{pmatrix}
=I.
\]
Since the matrix is invertible, its **Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse equals its inverse**:
\[
\boxed{A^+=A^{-1}=\frac17\begin{pmatrix}3&5\\-1&-4\end{pmatrix}.}
\]

