model_response:
The original answer is correct. To verify directly,
\[
\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\0&4\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}1&\tfrac12\\0&\tfrac14\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&\tfrac12-\tfrac12\\
0&1
\end{pmatrix}
=I.
\]
Thus
\[
\boxed{A^{-1}=A^{+}=\begin{pmatrix}1&\tfrac12\\0&\tfrac14\end{pmatrix}.}
\]
For an invertible square matrix, the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse is its ordinary inverse.

