I rechecked the calculation, and the original answer is correct. In fact,
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
17/6 & 1/2\\
-4 & 13/3
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
78 & -9\\
72 & 51
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
257 & 0\\
0 & 257
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Therefore,
\[
\boxed{A^{-1}=A^+=\frac1{257}
\begin{pmatrix}
78 & -9\\
72 & 51
\end{pmatrix}.}
\]
Since \(A\) is invertible, its Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse is the same as its inverse.

