model_response:
Yes—the inverse is correct. We can verify it by multiplication:
\[
\begin{pmatrix}4&5\\-4&0\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}0&-\frac14\\[2pt]\frac15&\frac15\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&-1+1\\
0&1
\end{pmatrix}
=I.
\]
Since the matrix is invertible, its Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse is the same:
\[
\boxed{A^+=A^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-\frac14\\[2pt]\frac15&\frac15\end{pmatrix}.}
\]

